WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.560
- First, let me introduce you

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.660
to the directors and the producer.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.930
So to my left, Lisa
Molmot, oh, sorry, Molomot

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.950
has directed and edited
award-winning documentaries

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.710
about the American
Southwest in recent years,

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.510
including "The Cleaners" and "Soledad".

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.360
She has also focused on
stories about education.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.190
Her award-winning film, "School's Out",

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.930
has been an integral part of the movement

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.870
for providing outdoor
education for young children,

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.580
and her short film, "Teaching in Arizona",

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.670
is an inside look at the
teaching crisis in the state.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.840
A recent Fulbright Scholar,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.440
she teaches courses at the
James E. Roger School of Law

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.330
at the University of Arizona,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.270
where she has worked with
the Immigration Law Clinic

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.190
for the past four years
and has also taught

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.223
in the UA Human Rights
Practice Graduate Program.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.120
Next to her is Jeff Bemiss,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.540
who is a producer, co-director,
and cinematographer.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.640
He is an award-winning writer/director

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.400
who has worked in shorts,
features, and documentaries.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.790
Jeff's work has aired on
network television and PBS.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:06.630
He is a graduate

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.270
of the University of Southern
California Film School

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.820
and the L.A. Sanford Meisner Academy.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.620
Originally trained in scripted filmmaking,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.180
Jeff's film, "The Book and The Rose",

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.190
was a semi-finalist for the Academy Award

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.660
for Best Short Film.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.367
Jeff shot and directed the
award-winning short documentary,

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:23.520
"Coaching Colburn", about a young man

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.860
with Fragile X Syndrome, which premiered

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.190
at the prestigious Big Sky
Documentary Film Festival.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:30.810
Jeff is a Connecticut Artist Fellow

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.790
and a Film Independent Fast Track Fellow.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.490
He freelances for disability
and social activist clients

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.140
and teaches film at Trinity College

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.850
in Hartford, Connecticut.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.660
And Jacob Bricca, who is producer/editor,

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.910
has edited over a dozen
feature documentaries,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.067
including the 2016 Sundance
Special Jury prize winner,

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:50.947
"The Bad Kids", and the
international theatrical hit,

00:01:50.947 --> 00:01:54.420
"Lost in LA", sorry, "Lost in La Mancha".

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.210
Fluent in Spanish, Jacob
also brings experience

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.030
editing multicultural stories
such as "Precious Knowledge",

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.470
about Arizona's epic battle

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.680
to ban ethnic studies in public schools;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.470
and "Beyond the Border" (speaks Spanish),

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.630
about four sons from a Mexican family

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.840
struggling to overcome
class barriers in Kentucky.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.090
Jacob has produced two
feature-length documentaries,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.610
is an associate professor
at the University of Arizona

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.620
School of Theatre, Film and Television,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.570
and is the author of the definitive volume

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.223
on documentary editing.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.010
So thank you for being here.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.550
And you know, I know the audience
might have some questions

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.230
about what they just saw on the screen,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.000
so I kind of wanted to ask some questions

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.820
that were more about, you
know, behind-the-scenes

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.800
and the process, right,
of making a documentary.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.670
So, you know, I'm particularly curious

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.878
about the various perspectives
that you manage together.

00:02:45.878 --> 00:02:47.700
And throughout the documentary,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.010
these perspectives map out the breakdown

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.190
in state and federal infrastructure,
and the varying roles

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.670
community members in Brooks
County take to fill in that gap.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.730
So how did you go about
establishing these relationships

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.620
with different members of the community,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.290
and how did these relationships

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.880
inform your research process,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.213
or what ended up making it on the screen?

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.433
- Is this on?

00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:16.440
Yeah, I'll say a few things
and then I can pass it to Jeff

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.440
and he can pass it to Jacob since, well,

00:03:19.455 --> 00:03:22.380
we probably all have something
to say about this, but

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.910
Jeff and I began filming this

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.410
in 2015.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.350
And I would say like the
first year of filming

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.930
was more research than anything else,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.260
because it was about
going to Brooks County

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.143
and understanding what was happening

00:03:39.143 --> 00:03:42.303
and forming relationships
with people there.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.380
And

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.060
I think the fact that the film is told

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.420
in so many different, from so
many different perspectives,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.570
one, we felt that that was necessary

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.130
to understand what was happening there,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.040
but also, you know, we were
just trying to figure out

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.250
what was happening there and to, you know,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.830
for a long time and
understand it ourselves.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.740
So I think in some way,
that's reflected in the film,

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.380
that we have all of these
different perspectives,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.060
but we actually couldn't stop filming.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.890
I mean, we were so shocked

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.570
when we first got to Brooks County

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.080
and saw what was happening
there, and we were...

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.200
The film sort of began
as a different story,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.660
a short film about something else.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.410
And within hours of
being in Brooks County,

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.520
we were like, "Okay, the
course of our film has changed,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.100
and we want to make a film
about what is happening here."

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.410
And just, it was a four-year journey

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:42.243
of documenting from all of
these different perspectives.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.610
- Yeah, we kind of, I mean, we joked

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.160
that every time we went
to Brooks County to film,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.070
the ending got further and further away

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.730
and we kept bringing more and
more footage back to Jacob.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.900
And sometimes people ask

00:05:00.088 --> 00:05:02.881
about the vigilante group

00:05:02.881 --> 00:05:07.500
as one group that, "How did
you get involved with them?"

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.200
And you know, Kate and
Eddie were different.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.033
They were kind of open,

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:13.710
because they were used
to talking to the media.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:18.030
But the vigilante group would,
kind of wanted some press,

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:22.380
but Michael Vickers would
meet us out somewhere.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.130
And it took us about,
really about three years

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.630
of nagging him and begging

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:31.470
to kind of go on one of these operations.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.210
And so that was a process.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.690
And I think each group had its own process

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.220
of building trust, but it was something

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:41.970
that we kind of had to start at zero with.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.090
And it was just by going back
and going back and going back,

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.367
and they would see that we
were shooting a lot of footage

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.260
and that we were working really hard.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.260
And I think that helped build the trust.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.930
- And anyone said there's
nothing we wouldn't shoot.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.500
So if that gives you, I mean,
we had a lot of footage.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.570
And I think in some ways,

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:04.241
some of it was just for our own purposes

00:06:04.241 --> 00:06:06.390
to get our heads around
what was happening.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.047
Even though sometimes we knew,

00:06:08.047 --> 00:06:09.897
"Okay, this is getting off-track,"

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.120
it still informed how we
understand what's happening

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.856
and talk about now, you know,

00:06:17.856 --> 00:06:19.822
what's happening in Brooks County, so,

00:06:19.822 --> 00:06:22.020
and all of Texas along the border.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.319
So yeah, it was a long, long journey.

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:28.620
- And the footage would end up with me

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.724
and I would look at it.

00:06:30.724 --> 00:06:34.350
And we sort of tried to start finding ways

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.060
to take these some, you know,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.330
in a way it's a series of
vignettes of different people,

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.270
and to find a way to put
them together in a way

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.610
that you saw the experience of one

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.010
kind of refracted in the other, in a way.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:50.073
So you just spend a little bit
of time with the individual

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:53.250
who is found, you know,
carrying a water jug,

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:55.440
and we learn a little bit about him.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.760
But that may be, you know,

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.760
it fills in an experience of
what that might be like, right,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.980
what that part of the
journey might be like

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.100
for somebody who, you know,
fortunately, is found.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.091
And so, you know, we had,

00:07:10.091 --> 00:07:13.800
by the time we came to
further in the editing,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.780
we had two main stories

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.120
and a lot of these other little vignettes,

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.830
and we just kept working
until we could figure out

00:07:21.688 --> 00:07:24.630
how to piece them all together.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.960
And obviously, it's Homero's
story who kind of anchors it.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.360
And his brother and
sister-in-law and family

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.350
kind of became partners
with us as we filmed,

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.540
and then also as we've taken the film out,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.360
they've been with us to
many, many, many screenings

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.210
talking about it.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:46.740
- So one of the fascinating
aspects about your documentary

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:47.820
is the context.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.350
You know, as you mentioned, you
started this project in 2015

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.052
and it first screened in 2020.

00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:59.100
I think that it's true in many
ways, not much has changed;

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.340
but I also think the opposite
is true, a lot has changed.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:07.340
And you know, between Trump's
2015 promise to build the wall

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.320
and the drastic changes
to immigration processes

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.880
caused by the COVID-19
emergency, things did change.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.870
So how did the context and
impact of the documentary change

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:23.853
between 2015 and 2020, as you
went through this process?

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.583
- Again, I'll start, and
just keep passing it.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:34.176
So, after Donald Trump was elected,

00:08:34.176 --> 00:08:37.470
I think the first thing we
noticed in Brooks County

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.590
was there was more law enforcement,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:44.610
more border patrol in Falfurrias
and around the checkpoint.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.473
And that has all remained.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.883
So that was like the most
obvious thing that changed.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.520
And I think, you know, it was interesting

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.380
'cause in the process of making the film,

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:01.950
you know, I mean five, four or five years

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.310
is a long time to make a documentary.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.270
And I was like, "You know, we
gotta get this film out there

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.500
so it's still relevant."

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.590
Like, at a certain point,
like things become irrelevant.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:18.150
But, and there's no way
we could have predicted

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.460
that the situation now,

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.460
given that 2022 was the
worst year on record

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.760
for migrant deaths along
the US-Mexico border.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.496
It is was by the United Nations IOM,

00:09:33.496 --> 00:09:36.540
International Organization on Migration,

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.970
they just came out very recently

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:40.920
stating that the US-Mexico border

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.990
is the deadliest border crossing,

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.093
land border crossing in the world,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.950
and last year was the
worst year on record.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.380
And I think starting in 2015,

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.443
I never would've thought things
would actually get worse.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:00.828
And now with climate
change, and you know...

00:10:00.828 --> 00:10:02.610
One of the reasons, one of the things

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.970
we don't state in the
film about the situation

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:10.170
is the reason why people
come in the hotter months,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.490
late spring and summer and
early fall in South Texas,

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:17.520
is because all of these
ranches are private

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.520
and what's happening on
the ranches is hunting.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:26.070
So migrants don't want to
come during hunting season,

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:30.063
which is the winter, which
would be much more sane.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.630
But, so they come when they can

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:35.730
and it's during the hottest months.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.670
And so every August, you know,

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:42.720
we predict there's gonna
be media saying, you know,

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.600
all about, talking about
all of these migrant deaths,

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.930
and you get a couple of articles
every August, late August,

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.810
and then it just goes away for a year.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.640
And then in August again,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.550
you see another "New York Times"
article, "Washington Post",

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:57.690
and then it goes away.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:59.880
And we've watched this for now how many,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.820
nine years, (scoffs)
basically the same thing.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.090
And nothing has changed,
except that it's gotten worse

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:11.090
in terms of the number of
remains that are found each year.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.090
- I don't really have
a lot to add to that,

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.590
except that the people that
we've sometimes partnered with

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.290
in the outreach and impact for this film,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.300
like the Washington
office and Latin America

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:25.830
and other groups and NGOs,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.490
they're not very happy with the
Biden administration either.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.810
Because really, as Lisa said,
the net effect is the same.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.790
Really, there's not much change.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:39.030
You know, the Title 42 ended,
but there's a transit ban,

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.180
and these things haven't really,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:43.470
nothing's really moved forward

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:47.400
in a positive direction significantly,

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.600
I think is fair to say.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.300
- [Lisa] Well, the Missing Persons Act.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.380
- Yeah, the one thing is, it's interesting

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.130
because with immigration, you
know, you see the headlines

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:02.610
and there's a lot of partisan sniping.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.610
But then you look back
at what's been passed,

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.800
and it really challenges your notion

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.570
of traditional left and right politics,

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.708
this issue of immigration.

00:12:11.708 --> 00:12:13.920
You know, Reagan gave amnesty,

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:16.890
and Bill Clinton, as you
know from the film now,

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.960
is really his administration
more responsible

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.660
for these deaths than any other.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:23.850
And yet the only president
to ever sign a bill

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:25.230
that took any responsibility

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.750
on behalf of the federal
government for these deaths

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.660
was Donald Trump, which he did

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:34.050
with the Missing Persons and
Unidentified Remains Act,

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.720
which he signed at the end of December,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:37.893
at the end of his term.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:39.693
So.

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.155
But you know, really the problem,

00:12:43.155 --> 00:12:46.050
the death rate continues to climb

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.783
and that's always been a constant, sadly.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.250
- So before I turn it to the audience,

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:54.360
I have one last question.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.360
So by this point, you've been
able to screen the documentary

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.220
and talk about it across different media,

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:03.240
across different audiences, PBS, podcasts,

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.910
academics, border patrol agents.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.440
Has the documentary's reception

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.563
by any of your audiences surprised you?

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.530
- Do you want to talk
about the Texas tour?

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:20.430
- Yeah, we've done a lot of impact,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.580
a lot of, many impact
screenings with the film.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.410
But one that sticks out in my mind

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:29.130
is Jeff and I, during the
height of the pandemic,

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.270
decided it was a good idea

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:32.400
to do a tour of Texas with this film.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.370
We did a bunch of mostly
outdoor screenings,

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:37.890
like we had a screen and a projector

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.183
and we went around Texas.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.170
And one of those screenings
was in San Antonio.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.163
And,

00:13:45.638 --> 00:13:50.010
there were some conservative
people in the audience

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:52.140
who, well, I know that from

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:57.140
just the questions they were
asking prior to the screening.

00:13:58.671 --> 00:13:59.973
And during the Q&amp;A,

00:14:01.414 --> 00:14:03.397
this woman stood up and said,

00:14:03.397 --> 00:14:07.920
"You know, this film really
has made me think a lot

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:10.470
about some of my views on immigration,

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.544
and thank you so much for telling,

00:14:13.544 --> 00:14:16.380
you know, bringing this
story to my attention

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.500
and this, you know, is gonna, you know,

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.750
give me something to chew on."

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:23.343
And you know,

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:28.860
we've been on conservative talk radio

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.933
with this, about this film.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:35.040
We have made a couple of trips to DC;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.510
we did a Senate hearing and
a Congressional hearing.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:43.320
We've, you know, we went
to Cuba with the film.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.240
We talked to people, young people

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.593
who are thinking about
immigrating to the US in Cuba.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.562
And it's all memorable because this film,

00:14:54.562 --> 00:14:57.300
it's just, it's so interesting

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:02.300
showing different audiences
the film and knowing that,

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:03.750
you know,

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.668
we're all human.

00:15:06.668 --> 00:15:08.730
This is not about politics.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.520
This is about human beings,
and everyone gets that.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:12.690
And it doesn't matter

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:15.420
whether I agree with
their politics or not,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:18.900
or grew up in the same
way that they did or not.

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:22.230
We're all kind of experiencing
this in the same way,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:25.230
which is just shock and
anger and, you know,

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:27.057
people ask, "What can I do?"

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:28.823
So.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.080
- Okay, thank you so much for that.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.450
I'm now going to turn it to the audience.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.780
We will have someone walking
around with a microphone,

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.910
so you know, you can just
go ahead and raise your hand

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.863
and a microphone will be passed to you.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.100
- [Leslie] Hi, thank you very much

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.680
for this really powerful film.

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.990
It's hard to even like
ask a question, (laughs)

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.093
I'm still processing so much about it.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.060
This is sort of out of left field,

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:18.600
but something I've been
thinking about a lot

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.560
in the context of my own work

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:26.910
with migrant people in
Mediterranean context.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.620
And I'm wondering about

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.560
the first, the opening
scenes of the human remains

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.780
made me think about these conversations

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:40.500
that are opening up again
regarding indigenous remains

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.360
that are housed in universities.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:43.440
And I was just thinking about

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.980
this sort of visual archive
of material that you have

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:47.973
and also,

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.150
you know, both in the film

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:52.560
and then in excess of that,

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.480
and how you might be thinking about that

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.870
in relationship to
questions about remains,

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.673
about repatriation, about,

00:17:01.439 --> 00:17:04.170
I don't know, accessibility of material,

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:06.000
like materials for people in the future

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:10.050
who might be looking through
that sort of material as well.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:11.670
If that's something that
you've thought about

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.263
or has come up at all for you.

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:18.690
- Leslie, you're giving me more work.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:22.305
Sorry.
(people laugh lightly)

00:17:22.305 --> 00:17:23.388
I don't know.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:28.350
- I mean, I do think that
Kate Spradley's group,

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:30.570
Operation Identification,

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:35.570
I do think that they collect
materials as they go along.

00:17:36.824 --> 00:17:38.790
I don't think we've ever
gotten that question.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:39.623
It's an excellent question.

00:17:39.623 --> 00:17:41.970
And I think that there,

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:46.350
I could envision a time
when OpID comes to us

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.450
and says, you know, "We're
starting a new project

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:51.990
and we're wondering if you
want to donate your footage,"

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.590
which of course, we'd be
happy to do, I'm sure.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.720
But yeah, I hadn't
really thought about it.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:58.553
- [Lisa] No.

00:18:00.996 --> 00:18:02.283
- [Leslie] Thank you.

00:18:11.207 --> 00:18:15.780
- It's a really moving film
and thank you for making it.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:20.780
I had a question about the
water, the barrels of water

00:18:20.859 --> 00:18:25.859
and where they were in
relationship to where people were,

00:18:26.827 --> 00:18:28.980
where people went missing,

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.320
versus people who succeeded
in getting through.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:35.610
When they took the, what it
was it 14, 15 barrels out,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:36.753
they disappeared,

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.480
I was wondering if that was also

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:43.890
from the vigilante group, on their land.

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:48.210
'Cause it, I wasn't sure which,
you put the Mariposa Ranch

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.730
and I didn't know whose ranch that was.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.740
So I'm just wondering about

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.700
where water didn't exist,

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:00.157
for instance, on that
one rancher who said,

00:19:00.157 --> 00:19:04.923
"Well, I have the water
source" for his cattle.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.370
Did the water barrels help

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.293
reduce the number of deaths in that area?

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.640
- So Eddie Canales, who is the, you know,

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:18.780
spearheads that project,

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.390
although as you can see, the other rancher

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:22.860
who you spend a tiny bit of time with,

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.207
who claims credit for the
being the (indistinct),

00:19:25.207 --> 00:19:28.743
he also does this on the
ranch that he operates.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:34.860
But Eddie Canales figures
that they are useful

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.410
because he does, you
know, replenish the water.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:38.760
And some, you know, he will,

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:42.300
every so often they go out
and you know, check them.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.330
And there are times when they've,

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.540
when the water's been taken
and they will then replace it.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:51.030
So you guys can help me on this.

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:53.040
I think it is really hard for him

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.320
to be able to document
X number of lives saved,

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.150
'cause how do you prove a negative?

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:58.230
- [Questioner] Well-
- Yeah.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:19:59.760
- [Questioner] What about
the reverse of that?

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:02.820
Where the bodies, where more
of the bodies have been found,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.710
is that where there are no rain barrels

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:08.400
because a rancher won't allow it?

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.520
- The roots are, and you guys
should correct me on this,

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.860
but as you can see from that map

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.720
that shows up before
they're doing that search,

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:16.830
there are a lot of ranches,

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:18.780
some of them are absolutely enormous.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:20.610
The Gage Ranch is one of them.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:22.830
And then there are other ones
that are relatively small,

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:25.680
and it is kind of a patchwork of the ones

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.370
that are where Eddie is allowed,

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.530
where they say, "Sure,
come and do your work

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:32.550
and put your barrels",

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.740
than the ones that he
does not have permission.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:38.430
And I don't know if, so it's
sort of like the patchwork

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.190
is a little too inconsistent

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:43.560
to be able to draw immediate conclusions

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.170
like the one that you're asking for.

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:49.860
But I think Eddie would probably
say he'll do it regardless

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.690
of whether, (chuckles) you know,

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.290
exactly what statistics
are shown or whatever.

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.140
And the fact that the water is being used

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.240
clearly on at least some occasions

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.850
is proof enough to him that that's true.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:05.807
And it is true that,
you know, the inference

00:21:05.807 --> 00:21:08.827
that Mike Vickers draws from this is,

00:21:08.827 --> 00:21:11.610
"Look, they're on the major routes,

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.550
and therefore he must be in
cahoots with them." (snickers)

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:16.860
They are on some of the major routes.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:21.480
So I think it's fair to say
that they are having an effect;

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:25.015
exactly what that is, is
kind of hard to determine.

00:21:25.015 --> 00:21:27.960
- And Eddie does use
the death mapping data

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.000
to choose where to put the water stations,

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.260
if a rancher will agree.

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:35.190
So, and of course, there's
snake bite and heat stroke,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:38.340
and sometimes when you reach
that level of dehydration,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:40.650
you know, the water may
not help at that point.

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:43.350
So, or they may not find
the barrels, or so forth.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.240
And so, it's very hard.

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.560
- And surprisingly in the winter,

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.810
I mean, there are fewer
migrants crossing, as Lisa said,

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.160
for that reason.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:51.390
And we did do a whole shoot

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:53.670
with people who are out
there hunting, (laughs)

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.110
which didn't make it into the film.

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:59.820
But there are, some deaths are
from exposure in the winter.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.240
You know, it's rainy and it
gets really cold out there.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.385
- And can I say one more thing
before the next question?

00:22:07.385 --> 00:22:09.090
Because I really don't want to leave here

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:10.090
without saying this.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:12.990
But regarding your comment

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:16.680
about seeing the Mariposa Ranch sign,

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.500
I think that might be the only ranch sign

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.360
we put in the film.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:26.080
And it just reminds me to say
that the Yale Docuproject,

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:31.320
which is a media law clinic,

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:36.320
took this project on pro bono
as an assignment in a course,

00:22:36.707 --> 00:22:40.440
and they went through
the film scene by scene

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:44.910
and gave us legal advice,
or sort of gave us advice

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.880
about anything that seemed questionable

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:52.880
or could be potentially
problematic legally.

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:57.870
And one of the things after
this three-hour consultation,

00:22:57.870 --> 00:22:59.430
very detailed consultation,

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:02.280
very scary consultation that we did,

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:06.090
was we took out the ranch names
for almost all the ranches.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:08.520
I think we had a bunch
of other signs in there,

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.280
and we took them all out
except for the Mariposa,

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:14.760
because we felt that
was part of the story,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.493
like that was necessary

00:23:16.493 --> 00:23:18.960
because we kept going
back to that same ranch

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:21.300
and people were referring to the ranch.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.130
So we left it in.

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:27.030
We did get permission also
on that ranch to film.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:30.480
I think we felt more solid
on that particular ranch.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:34.800
The other ranches, we got
permission from the managers,

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.720
but we learned that that's not enough.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.820
It's actually the owner
that needs to give you,

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.310
so in case you're ever
thinking of doing this,

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.810
there's a bunch of documentary
students out there.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:48.720
So I just wanted to like give
a shout-out to that clinic

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:53.720
because that was a real
education that, you know,

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:57.451
and it was all pro bono and just amazing.

00:23:57.451 --> 00:23:59.340
- Yeah, we owe them a huge debt.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:00.840
They were really wonderful.

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.760
And then the other part of
your question real quick

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.250
is those barrels that were taken,

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:06.750
they were outside the ranch gates,

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:07.980
so those were on a public road.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:09.870
I mean, it was out in
the middle of nowhere,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.540
but anybody could have just
driven up and grabbed those.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.570
And Eddie has his suspicions,
which I will not repeat,

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.570
but we don't know where, but
it's not easy to take 'em.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:20.100
I mean, they gotta take those,

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:23.460
they took the poles out
of the ground and I mean,

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:25.365
it would be very difficult
to take 15 of those,

00:24:25.365 --> 00:24:28.174
one person.
- [Lisa] And hide them.

00:24:28.174 --> 00:24:30.224
- [Nancy] There's a question in the back.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:36.750
- [Questioner 2] Thanks,
thanks for the opportunity

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.883
to talk to you about this film.

00:24:40.272 --> 00:24:43.230
I have two parts to my question.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.070
They're kind of different.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:45.780
So just in the interest of time,

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:46.890
I'll just put them both out there,

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:51.660
and then if you'd like to
respond to either one of them.

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:54.900
One of them is about the
making of documentary.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.140
So my class at Wesleyan had
the opportunity yesterday

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:02.310
to hear from Jacob about
the making of documentary

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:04.410
and narrative and narrativity.

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:07.410
I wondered, you know,
on one level I wondered

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:10.530
if in the working on
this film, if it affected

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:15.023
or how it affected your
thinking about documentary

00:25:15.023 --> 00:25:16.470
as a media, you know,

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.432
in terms of just any thoughts about that

00:25:19.432 --> 00:25:22.860
in terms of the edits you're
making and then the choices.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:25.080
The other question is
about the subject matter.

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:29.310
So I direct a Center for the
Study of Guns and Society

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:30.150
at Wesleyan.

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:30.983
And,

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:36.900
you know, I was really struck
by the scenes around hunting,

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:41.880
and I think this kind of way
that hunting and surveillance

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.070
are also getting closer together.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.530
In fact, I think there was a
court case about five years ago

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:50.070
where a ranch had to shut down

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:53.700
because of a injunction
because they were using...

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:58.290
What they were doing was
encouraging kind of safari hunting,

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:00.600
hunting of animals on the ranch property

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.800
as a way to make money,

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:05.760
but people could hunt from
their computers in New Jersey.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:09.738
And so the technology of
doing surveillance at night

00:26:09.738 --> 00:26:11.280
for the purpose of hunting.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:13.650
And when you said that in the winter,

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:17.400
the migrants aren't crossing
because of the fear of hunting,

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:19.290
just also those scenes of the animals.

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.890
I guess my question was
if, I mean a lot, you know,

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:25.380
given how few of the missing
people have been recovered,

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.320
and mostly when they are the
finding is of dehydration,

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:33.320
but listening to the, you know,
some of the armed vigilantes

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.670
and just tracking that
a little bit myself,

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.230
I was curious if you

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:39.660
or any of the people you talked with there

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.723
thought that there also
is lynching happening.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:45.567
- Do one of you want to
take the first question

00:26:45.567 --> 00:26:46.400
and then I'll...

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.653
I mean, the second question,
then I'll take the first?

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.810
- Well, there was a case of lynching,

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.340
but I can't remember the details.

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:58.590
Do you remember the details of that?

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:01.353
But I, yeah, I can't remember, but.

00:27:04.995 --> 00:27:07.912
The vigilantes did run into some problems

00:27:07.912 --> 00:27:11.910
before we were filming with
them, for detaining people,

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:13.910
which they're not legally allowed to do.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:17.430
So there's some question

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:20.940
about whether there was the
camera effect with us around,

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:23.370
and maybe they cleaned up
their act a little bit.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.890
I was a little surprised
who those people were.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:28.920
I mean, I thought it would sort
of be any cowboy with a gun,

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:29.910
you know.

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:32.651
But they were NASA engineers,

00:27:32.651 --> 00:27:35.882
they were retired judges, politicians.

00:27:35.882 --> 00:27:38.400
And they would, people would come

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:41.823
and spend their vacations
sometimes doing this,

00:27:42.658 --> 00:27:45.974
tracking migrants on these ranches.

00:27:45.974 --> 00:27:50.900
There was this weird
sporting element to it and,

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:54.240
you know, they were ostensibly
trying to stop traffic

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:55.800
from coming through.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:57.900
But if nothing happened that night,

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:59.340
they were so disappointed.

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:03.900
And that was just again, this sort of,

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:07.680
this distasteful sporting vibe

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:09.030
to the whole thing.

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:12.120
- And I mean, they were
also proud of the lives

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:16.050
that they said they had
saved, in some cases.

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:17.760
Because, you know, as you can see,

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:20.730
if at that point you do want
to be found by somebody,

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:23.430
presuming that they will end
up (chuckles) returning you

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:26.130
to shelter and water and such.

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:29.223
But yeah, it was definitely a weird vibe.

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:33.300
And your first question
was sort of about making,

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:35.400
putting together the documentary.

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:39.780
And you know, this is,
again, this film is sort of,

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.670
it's an amalgam experience.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:43.799
We stayed pretty close to the,

00:28:43.799 --> 00:28:45.840
within each of the two stories,

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:48.510
we were very faithful to, I mean,

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:50.280
of course you have to
take creative license

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:52.830
with precisely when you tell what,

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:56.580
but we were very faithful to
the facts as they occurred.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:58.770
You know, so you are there the moment

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:02.700
when Juan Jr. was found, just
like it's shown, et cetera.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:04.410
But then there are all
the other little vignettes

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:06.147
that play around in there.

00:29:06.147 --> 00:29:09.360
And so for instance, the
vigilantes are out there at night,

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:11.730
they think they see
someone, the scene ends,

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:14.250
and then there's just
an interstitial moment

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:16.200
where you just see brush at night.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:19.980
And then you're in the car
with the sheriff's deputy

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:21.720
who finds someone on the road.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:25.117
And there was, we had
advice from someone like,

00:29:25.117 --> 00:29:27.060
"Just put those scenes right together,"

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.490
they make the call and here's
the guy and he comes out,

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.050
even though there were different evenings.

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:32.970
And we had it that way for a little bit,

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:36.087
and then we decided, "Well,
that is pretty misleading,"

00:29:36.087 --> 00:29:38.640
that it didn't occur that
way that night, right?

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:40.530
And that it doesn't necessarily make

00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:42.570
that much difference in the story.

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:45.930
So that was the reason why that
shot is in the middle there,

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:47.970
which is just a shot of things at night,

00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:49.560
and then we cut to a new scene.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.050
And so it's kind of
ambiguous, or I don't know,

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:53.640
you can tell me how you interpreted it.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.237
But we invite the interpretation of,

00:29:55.237 --> 00:29:56.850
"Look, they were out looking for someone,

00:29:56.850 --> 00:29:58.890
here's who they found,"

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:01.230
and yet we're not directly saying

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:02.880
that that is what happened.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:06.120
But on an amalgam level, that
is what we're saying happens.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.730
Because they are alleging
that these are terrorists

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.470
and they're coming to town

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:13.320
to overthrow the
government from the inside.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:15.210
And our experience from being there,

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:17.370
our meaning their experience,

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.980
they're the ones who shot
over this amount of time,

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:20.970
was that it's much more,

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.310
if you had to put it into one story,

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:27.960
this, you know, Miguel who
was, with the wet socks,

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:30.420
that is a pretty typical event, right?

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:32.760
That is the kind of person
who is found out there.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:34.890
So it's sort of choices like that.

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:37.410
And then other choices
where we were really,

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:40.170
we wanted to make you feel
like you had been out there.

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:42.120
So there are moments in which we,

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.430
that are scenes that are
relatively leisurely,

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:47.550
where you just sort of sit
and you listen to the sounds.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:49.800
And that was really important to us,

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:54.330
to feel like you could feel
like it might've felt out there.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:55.740
And they would come back

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:58.770
talking about the sounds
(laughs) of the brush.

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.360
And that's why we ended,

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:02.670
and we also, like for
instance, at the end,

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:04.620
we always assumed there'd
be an ending song.

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:08.100
We had money in the budget
to license something.

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:10.590
We tried out, I don't know
how many different songs,

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:13.410
and it never, nothing
ever felt appropriate.

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:16.710
And there's a lot of music in the film.

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:18.630
I hope it's relatively subtle and such.

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:21.180
But when we went to the
credits, it always felt wrong.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:22.860
And so we were like,
"Why do we need music?"

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:24.270
It's much more appropriate

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:27.300
to just be out there in
the brush with those sounds

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:28.350
and just let them play.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:31.650
It felt more respectful, it
felt more appropriate, you know.

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:33.000
It wasn't, you didn't get

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:36.028
like sort of an uplift moment, but.

00:31:36.028 --> 00:31:38.940
- Well also it forces you, the viewer,

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:41.070
to continue thinking
about what you just saw

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:44.310
rather than have some kind
of music be in your head

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:45.210
and you've moved on.

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:47.670
I think that's usually with credit music,

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:50.100
it's sort of to kind of jolt
you out of the experience

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:53.340
of watching a film, and
we did not want that.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.440
We want people to (throat clears)

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:56.788
reflect on what they just saw

00:31:56.788 --> 00:32:00.630
and have all of the
emotions that one might have

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:02.430
after seeing something like this, so.

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:03.570
- And we hoped that there,

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:05.610
I mean, we feel like
there is hope in the film.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:07.620
There's quite a bit of hope with the fact

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:10.530
that people are out there
caring as much as they do

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:14.760
and you know, spending
in, I don't know, there's,

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:15.960
we hope it's not as bleak

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:19.670
as some moments might make it feel.

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.553
But it also, you know, like to...

00:32:22.553 --> 00:32:27.453
Yeah, I mean, it's a film
about death, so (laughs) yeah.

00:32:29.611 --> 00:32:34.179
- So I have a question that
came from a viewer on Zoom

00:32:34.179 --> 00:32:36.870
and this is a follow-up
to the audience question.

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:39.750
So they want to know who
was your intended audience,

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:41.640
and you know, did you hope to reach

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:44.820
or challenge conservative
audiences or points of view

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.023
with your documentary?

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:50.310
- I'll start, and.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:55.310
Yeah, I mean, so we decided to get,

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:58.840
well, decided, we were very
lucky to get funding from PBS

00:32:59.952 --> 00:33:01.740
toward the end of the process.

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.410
And that means, getting funding from PBS

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:06.509
means the film will broadcast on PBS,

00:33:06.509 --> 00:33:08.610
not HBO, not, you know.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:11.998
And one of the great things about PBS

00:33:11.998 --> 00:33:15.930
is the audience is half
liberal, half conservative,

00:33:15.930 --> 00:33:19.470
and that just felt like the
right thing for this film.

00:33:19.470 --> 00:33:24.470
So in terms of target audience,
anyone that will watch,

00:33:24.694 --> 00:33:27.690
anyone that will watch
and listen, you know.

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:31.203
I'll talk to anyone about
this film and you know, so.

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:35.167
Usually like I always
tell my students like,

00:33:35.167 --> 00:33:36.780
"You need to have a
target audience." (laughs)

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:40.140
Like, and I don't know necessarily,

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:43.500
I mean, we're just as happy
to share about this film

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:47.040
with a church group as we are, you know,

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:49.803
we showed the film to
border patrol leaders.

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:52.980
They weren't necessarily our intended,

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:54.840
I wasn't thinking about
border patrol leaders

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.340
when we made this film,

00:33:56.340 --> 00:33:58.920
but certainly like in
the back of our minds,

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:01.590
like of course, we want to show
it to border patrol leaders.

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:05.333
So I, you know, did we have
a target audience, Jeff?

00:34:05.333 --> 00:34:09.810
- I mean, we did talk about
making a 360 degree portrait

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:11.970
of what was happening in Brooks County.

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:14.700
We wanted to treat the
audience like intelligent,

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:16.500
capable of making up their own minds

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:21.210
about what kind of policies
should administer the border.

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:23.610
But in order to do that, you
have to know what's going on,

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:24.443
you have to witness.

00:34:24.443 --> 00:34:26.700
And so I think sometimes we were thinking

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:28.560
we just want people to see this.

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.053
And I think we also felt that,

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.520
if people could meet the
families of the missing,

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:36.630
they would feel differently

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:38.370
about the way we're
administering our borders.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:40.320
Certainly, lawmakers
would feel differently

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.263
about the policies that they are enacting.

00:34:46.636 --> 00:34:48.240
- [Nancy] You have a question back there?

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:49.140
- [Questioner 3] Yeah, I have a question.

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:50.790
It was an amazing film, you guys.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:53.760
Thank you so much for bringing it here.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:56.370
I'm from Arizona, so it's
very dear to my heart.

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:58.830
Y'all are my professors,
so, you know. (laughs)

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:01.470
Just seeing this, I've spent
a lot of time in Douglas,

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.430
and so I'm very familiar

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:05.070
with the border situation in Arizona.

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:06.270
And one of the things

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:08.040
that I really appreciated you guys doing

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:11.130
was bringing all aspects of
how people become missing,

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:16.130
whether that's death or
dehydration or the unknown.

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:20.010
And so I was just wondering
if you guys had any stories,

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:20.843
I know that you guys

00:35:20.843 --> 00:35:22.170
probably had a lot of different stories

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:23.850
and you had to be very selective

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:25.680
of which ones you included in the film,

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:27.390
but were there any stories of people

00:35:27.390 --> 00:35:30.120
who were missing and were found,

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:33.900
or you know, just in a different
state, anything like that?

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:37.110
And I just really wanted
to also point out,

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.690
Bricca, you already did,
that the ending was silent

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:41.910
and just the sounds of the nature.

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:44.070
I really appreciated that.

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:45.720
It was almost like a moment of respect

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:48.450
for the people who are
still missing or lost.

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:50.310
And so I just, before you mentioned it,

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:53.340
I wanted to bring up that it
was a very beautiful moment.

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:54.640
I really appreciated that.

00:35:57.750 --> 00:36:00.135
Oh, so my question was if
you guys had any people that-

00:36:00.135 --> 00:36:01.285
- Were found.
(group laughs)

00:36:01.285 --> 00:36:02.702
Were found, yeah.

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:05.522
- I mean, there were a lot.

00:36:05.522 --> 00:36:06.783
We shot a lot of footage,

00:36:06.783 --> 00:36:09.600
so there were a lot of
stories, you're right.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:12.720
- And there are, you know,
as the title card says,

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:15.210
there had been quite a few cases

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.600
in which Kate Spradley's
group have been successful.

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:19.620
- Yeah, they're up to 100.

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:20.793
So they passed 100, so.

00:36:20.793 --> 00:36:23.370
- And you know, one sort
of interesting thing

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:28.170
is that actually the way that
this sort of process works

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:32.370
of recovery and the coordination
between agencies and such

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:35.190
is actually much, much, much
more functional in Arizona

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:36.540
than it is in Texas.

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:39.570
So their rate is much, much, much higher.

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:40.830
The kind of percentage of

00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:44.910
if you were to go missing
in Arizona and die,

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:47.400
whether your identity would be found

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:49.380
and you know, your
remains would be reunited,

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.589
is just, it's just a different system.

00:36:52.589 --> 00:36:55.080
Texas is highly decentralized.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:57.270
A lot of these counties
don't have much of a budget

00:36:57.270 --> 00:37:00.630
or much expertise, are not
actually following the law,

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:02.220
there's no coordination,

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:05.280
there's confusion about responsibility.

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:07.020
And as you hear in that story,

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:10.327
even sometimes the funeral
homes are kind of, you know,

00:37:10.327 --> 00:37:11.340
"Sure, we'll take your money,

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:13.410
and then we'll just pocket the money."

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:16.500
And you know, so there's...

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:19.860
And Brooks County is now
one of the better places

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:20.940
in terms of the way it's run.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:23.760
But that's partly because
of sort of, you know,

00:37:23.760 --> 00:37:25.830
expose on previous practices, so.

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:30.660
- And so, in Arizona, we've
got a medical examiner,

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:32.400
I live in Tucson,

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:34.560
medical examiner and an NGO

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:36.750
called the Colibrí
Center for Human Rights,

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:38.490
and they are in the same building.

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:42.673
And they meet weekly and
they compare the remains,

00:37:42.673 --> 00:37:44.400
the DNA from the remains,

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:48.360
and then the phone calls
from the families, you know,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:50.640
DNA from the family members,

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:54.240
and they can put these two
things together and make matches.

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:56.580
And they do, and it's very successful.

00:37:56.580 --> 00:38:00.810
And as Jacob mentioned, in
Texas, that doesn't exist.

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:03.060
It's the wild west in Texas.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:05.220
There's no centralized system.

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:08.970
And one of the reasons why
we've been to DC with the film

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:11.684
was to get NGOs like,

00:38:11.684 --> 00:38:15.046
you know, what's happening
at Texas State funding

00:38:15.046 --> 00:38:16.800
to do what they're doing.

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:17.940
They're, you know,

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.970
they're not funded by
the federal government.

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:22.560
They're writing grants.

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:26.760
They're doing this work as part
of research at a university.

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:30.909
So that has been a goal,

00:38:30.909 --> 00:38:32.790
one goal of this film

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:35.970
is to help them, support them,

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:40.290
in this centralized system for Texas.

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:44.460
- And in fact, there were
two more stories we filmed

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:46.680
that didn't make it into the film.

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:49.830
One of them, the... and
both were identifications

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:53.940
that were done by Operation
Identification, OpID.

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:58.248
And one of them, the family
decided in the middle

00:38:58.248 --> 00:39:02.217
that they just weren't ready,
which is understandable.

00:39:02.217 --> 00:39:04.710
This is not for everyone.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:06.330
And the other story,

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:09.780
I think we just couldn't find
a place for it in the film.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:12.060
It was just, it was a
really complicated film

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:13.680
with a lot of stories.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:15.686
- [Lisa] And it was a complicated story.

00:39:15.686 --> 00:39:19.683
- It was, it was compelling,
it was a very compelling story.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:28.320
A sister had died, and
then her living sister,

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:31.626
who was also undocumented,
was kind of stuck

00:39:31.626 --> 00:39:34.440
between what they kind of
call two borders, you know,

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:37.860
the interior checkpoint and
then the US-Mexico border.

00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:39.570
It's an area that sometimes is referred to

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:40.770
as the golden cage.

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:42.900
And she actually could, it was really sad,

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:45.930
she actually could not go
to her sister's funeral

00:39:45.930 --> 00:39:48.160
because it was held
north of the checkpoint

00:39:49.299 --> 00:39:52.710
where, you know, she had
known people and had,

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:56.160
there was family and she was, couldn't,

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.830
she was afraid to cross the
checkpoint that had, you know,

00:39:58.830 --> 00:40:01.350
resulted in her sister's
demise, of course.

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:05.447
So it was just too complicated
to put in the film.

00:40:05.447 --> 00:40:06.870
- And in addition to that,

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:09.450
she was in a domestic violence situation,

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:13.350
where she couldn't leave this situation

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:16.950
because she was stuck
between these two borders

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:20.010
and didn't have family around.

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:22.287
And eventually, I believe,

00:40:22.287 --> 00:40:25.441
the authorities let her go to Houston

00:40:25.441 --> 00:40:27.480
because of this domestic violence.

00:40:27.480 --> 00:40:29.910
But for years, she was
living in this situation.

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:33.051
We met her, we talked with
her, we filmed with her.

00:40:33.051 --> 00:40:35.610
And so it's like not, you know,

00:40:35.610 --> 00:40:39.450
like this issue of these
interior checkpoints,

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:43.711
they have so many consequences,
even beyond death.

00:40:43.711 --> 00:40:45.480
They create these situations

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:47.850
where people can't get medical assistance,

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:51.330
people can't get out of
an abusive relationship,

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:53.490
people, you know, can't go to a funeral.

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:57.303
It's like, those are
just a few things, but.

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:02.463
- [Nancy] And there is
one question back here.

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:09.517
- Thank you so much for this film.

00:41:09.517 --> 00:41:14.517
This was like very hard-hitting
and like beautiful to see.

00:41:15.493 --> 00:41:17.040
I have two questions.

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.887
One, as you mentioned, like you went there

00:41:19.887 --> 00:41:22.170
and you discovered for yourself as well.

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.330
So, like curious if you
could share a little bit

00:41:24.330 --> 00:41:27.930
about how it evolved over time.

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:30.210
And as you mentioned, you
could take in some stories

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:33.184
but could not do the others.

00:41:33.184 --> 00:41:36.060
And the second is how like you,

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:38.310
it's amazing how you build those relations

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:39.780
and that trust with people, right,

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:41.940
from the authorities
to the vigilante groups

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:44.340
to even more importantly, the families

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:47.580
who like went through
a lot of like turmoil

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:49.650
and it was not like a story for them.

00:41:49.650 --> 00:41:54.150
So curious about how you
kind of dealt with that.

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:56.340
And also you must have like shot so much

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:58.710
like in the car and everywhere.

00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:01.530
Like, was there something as a filmmaker

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:04.050
that you were thinking about managing?

00:42:04.050 --> 00:42:06.780
Like how did you manage filming,

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:09.000
like going out to the field, the desert,

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:12.692
when they were like
identifying the missing people

00:42:12.692 --> 00:42:15.480
or like even the different stories?

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:19.980
Like, I'm just curious about
how that process was for you.

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:22.920
- I'll say about working
with the families,

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:25.260
just one anecdote and
then they can talk about

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:26.510
what it was like filming.

00:42:27.599 --> 00:42:30.750
Omar and Michelle, who
are the married couple,

00:42:30.750 --> 00:42:34.800
and it's his brother and her
brother-in-law who is missing,

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:35.910
they found us.

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:38.340
So we had a website
before the film was done

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:40.380
called MissingInBrooksCounty.com.

00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:43.020
And right around the point in which,

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:45.300
of course it had been two
years, all of this had happened,

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:47.167
and yet they were sort
of back to thinking,

00:42:47.167 --> 00:42:49.320
"No, we don't want to give up."

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:52.320
Michelle googled "Missing
Brooks County Texas"

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:54.240
and she came up with our film.

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:57.030
And she just wrote us on
the little contact form

00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:00.150
and we immediately put her
in touch with Eddie Canales.

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:03.120
But they were also about
to make a trip to Texas

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.670
and they said, "Would you be
interested in meeting with us?"

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:08.147
And they really were quite ready.

00:43:08.147 --> 00:43:12.180
They were, very much
wanted their story told.

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:14.430
And that was how that came about.

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:16.020
It was a weird, that's not...

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:17.730
We weren't soliciting people or anything,

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:20.377
but that's how that story actually,

00:43:20.377 --> 00:43:24.573
that's how our involvement
with them started.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:30.060
- And for the documentary
filmmaking students out there,

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:30.933
I see you all,

00:43:32.753 --> 00:43:34.740
I have to be honest.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:36.960
When we got that email from Michelle,

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:39.330
I was like, "Not another family!"

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:41.910
Like, "We're already
filming with two families,

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:45.750
and you know, this film
is getting out of control.

00:43:45.750 --> 00:43:48.780
We've got the vigilantes,
we've got the..." you know.

00:43:48.780 --> 00:43:52.620
And, but, you know, deep inside,

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:55.380
you have to explore every possibility,

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:57.676
even though I was overwhelmed

00:43:57.676 --> 00:44:00.090
by the directions that we were going,

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:01.860
all the different
directions we were going in.

00:44:01.860 --> 00:44:03.543
And so,

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:07.237
we wrote back and said, you know,

00:44:07.237 --> 00:44:10.800
"We're coming to Texas next
week, like, can we meet?"

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:12.960
And we did, and it was amazing,

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:17.223
because I think very, very
quickly, we met in Starbucks,

00:44:18.608 --> 00:44:23.608
we knew very quickly that this
was gonna be a good match,

00:44:24.771 --> 00:44:27.600
I think in part because
it had been two years,

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:30.990
it wasn't as raw as some
of the other families.

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:33.450
But also, you know, we were careful.

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:35.940
We were like, "Look, go back as a family,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:37.890
talk to your parents, talk through this,

00:44:37.890 --> 00:44:42.030
see if this feels like a
good thing for your family."

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:44.310
And like, I don't know,
within hours we heard back

00:44:44.310 --> 00:44:47.520
that they were very much
wanting to participate

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:51.300
and then we just began
filming probably the next day.

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:55.627
But my instinct at first
was like, "Oh no," you know.

00:44:55.627 --> 00:44:57.450
"It's like, this is so complicated,

00:44:57.450 --> 00:44:58.800
like too many families, too many."

00:44:58.800 --> 00:44:59.910
I mean we've...

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:03.030
But to answer your
question, first question,

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:07.470
like you know, we did, we
have footage of other crossers

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:10.800
that we, you know, met that
were, that had given up,

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:14.490
you know, and from Honduras
and you know, all over.

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:16.980
It just, we have a lot of footage

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.993
that we never put into the film.

00:45:19.950 --> 00:45:23.430
And so, but you know,
just a word, you know,

00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:26.130
just a lesson learned, you
know, for myself as well,

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:30.810
is that you need to
pursue all different leads

00:45:31.676 --> 00:45:36.270
and see where they go,
as difficult as it seems.

00:45:36.270 --> 00:45:37.620
'Cause it can be very overwhelming,

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:40.110
having hundreds of hours of
footage and knowing, you know,

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:42.420
I come from an editing background,

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:43.950
knowing that someone has to like,

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:46.830
sift through all this footage
and make sense of it all.

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:50.460
And so, at a certain point,
you have to discriminate.

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.190
Yeah, but it was just, it was Jacob,

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:53.481
so I was like, "Okay, let's do it."

00:45:53.481 --> 00:45:54.537
(group laughs lightly)

00:45:54.537 --> 00:45:57.720
- And I mean, there are some
types of documentary films

00:45:57.720 --> 00:46:01.560
that you really can research
and prosecute in a way

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:04.200
that allows you to be pretty
targeted about, you know,

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:07.282
you can search out
experts to do interviews

00:46:07.282 --> 00:46:10.710
and lead people through things in a way

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:12.870
where your research will lead
you to what you want to shoot

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:14.970
and it's kind of straightforward.

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:17.850
And then there is sort of
the cinéma vérité ideal,

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:21.300
which is you learn as you go, (chuckles)

00:46:21.300 --> 00:46:22.890
and you shoot a lot of stuff,

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:24.450
and you kind of make sense of it

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:26.880
as you look at what
you've gathered, in a way.

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:30.480
It's a much more sort
of far-flung and organic

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:33.090
kind of searching that you're doing,

00:46:33.090 --> 00:46:34.680
that you're living inside of.

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:38.710
And that was what more interesting
to all of us, even though

00:46:42.907 --> 00:46:44.207
it's harder to know that you're
going in the right direction

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:48.240
or that you know exactly what you're doing

00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:49.350
in any given moment.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:52.590
It starts to become clearer as
you start to form conclusions

00:46:52.590 --> 00:46:54.030
about what it is that you've shot.

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:57.330
And in retrospect, like,
okay, yeah, it's a mosaic.

00:46:57.330 --> 00:46:59.010
There are vignettes, you know,

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:00.600
it sounds easy to talk about.

00:47:00.600 --> 00:47:02.373
But as you're making it, it can be,

00:47:03.210 --> 00:47:05.700
you are sort of struggling to figure out

00:47:05.700 --> 00:47:07.290
how the meaning that you're finding in it

00:47:07.290 --> 00:47:10.680
can be put together in a way

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:14.193
that will express that
kind of meaning, so.

00:47:15.814 --> 00:47:18.180
- So I think we're reaching time.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:20.940
I just want to say thank you
so much for this conversation,

00:47:20.940 --> 00:47:23.134
and any last remarks,

00:47:23.134 --> 00:47:25.443
or anything that you
want to leave us with?

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:29.040
- I'd like to thank everyone

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:32.100
who's responsible for bringing
us here, a lot, it's been-

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:34.860
- Theresa.
(group chuckles)

00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:35.693
- Okay, thank you so much.
(audience applauds)

00:47:35.693 --> 00:47:37.020
- Thank you.
(audience applauds)

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:39.130
- Charlie Musser, from film studies.

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:39.990
(audience applauds)

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:41.580
- Thank you for those of you

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:43.290
who've joined us online right now,

00:47:43.290 --> 00:47:45.293
we know there are those folks out there.

00:47:46.425 --> 00:47:48.842
(soft music)

